Subject: JSON.parse matches an expected key with one memcmp and scans string bodies sixteen bytes at a time

What this does
  - `JSONShapeCache.plain`: per cached key, whether its atom is an 8-bit string of bytes a JSON key holds unescaped (`json_atom_plain`, computed once when the key enters the chain); only such keys are passed as the expected-key hint, and `json_parse_key_string` then needs only the closing quote and a memcmp
  - `json_scan_plain`: the end of a run a JSON string copies as it is (quote, backslash, control character, a byte >= 0x80 unless Latin-1 input), sixteen bytes at a time with SSE2, never reading past the input's end; used by both loops of `json_parse_string`

Why we need it
  After 0587 the key match (a five-condition test per byte) and the byte-at-a-time string scan were 20% of parse cycles on the app-traffic benchmark. json_app parse only: -6.0% cycles, -6.6% instructions.

Soundness
  A plain key's bytes are each accepted by the general key scan, so equal input bytes followed by a quote are a key the scan would read as the same atom. The vector test is the scalar one (signed compare below 0x20 catches control characters and bytes >= 0x80; the Latin-1 form masks the latter out) and the first set bit is the first such byte; the loop runs only while sixteen bytes remain. Differentials identical: 49,200 strings and keys with each special byte (quote, backslash, escape, 0x01, 0x1f, 0x7f, 0x80, é, ÿ, €) at every position of lengths 0-39, complete and truncated; and all earlier JSON differentials.

